<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Boccaccio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1313">1313</a>–1375</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Boccaccio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Boccaccio (1313–1375) was an Italian writer and humanist, born in or near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, who spent his youth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in the circle of the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert of Anjou</a>. His Decameron, a collection of a hundred tales told by young Florentines escaping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348, is a masterpiece of Italian prose. A friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a>, he also wrote a life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> and lectured publicly on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio.jpg" alt="Giovanni Boccaccio" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea del Castagno · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_del_Castagno_Giovanni_Boccaccio_c_1450.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boccaccio was the son of the Florentine merchant Boccaccino di Chellino and an unknown woman, and was probably born out of wedlock. He grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, where his father worked for the Compagnia dei Bardi, and may have been tutored by Giovanni Mazzuoli, who gave him an early introduction to the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>. In 1326 his father was appointed head of a bank and the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Boccaccio disliked banking and studied canon law at the Studium for six years, while pursuing scientific and literary studies and meeting scholars and the Neapolitan nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Naples Boccaccio began what he considered his true vocation, poetry. Il Filostrato and the Teseida of this period were the sources for Chaucer's Troilus and Criseyde and The Knight's Tale. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in early 1341, after tensions between the Angevin king and Florence, and his father, who had gone bankrupt, died in 1349. He began the Decameron around 1349; the choice of a hundred tales and the frame story of three men and seven women dates from this time. It was largely complete by 1352 and revised in 1370-1371.</p><p>From 1350 he was closely involved with humanism and with the Florentine government, which sent him on missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>. He housed Leontius Pilatus and encouraged his tentative translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>. In October 1350 he welcomed Francesco Petrarch to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and Petrarch became his teacher and friend. Their discussions fed the Genealogia deorum gentilium, completed in 1360, a reference work on classical mythology that defended the study of ancient literature against clerical intellectuals who wished to limit it. In 1360 he began De mulieribus claris, biographies of 106 women, completed in 1374, and in 1373 he lectured on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>, which produced his Esposizioni sopra la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Commedia</a> di Dante.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Illness troubled Boccaccio's last years. He died on 21 December 1375 in Certaldo, where he is buried, and his library passed to the monastery of Santo Spirito in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. He is counted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> as one of the Three Crowns of Italian literature. Pietro Bembo made his style a model of Italian prose in the sixteenth century, and he influenced Chaucer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega">Lope de Vega</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giovanni Boccaccio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Robert the Wise the Angevin court attracted Giotto, who worked there around 1328–1333, and Petrarch, who was examined by the king in 1341 before his coronation as poet laureate in Rome, while the young <b>Giovanni Boccaccio</b> spent formative years in the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Dante’s Divine Comedy, together with the works of Petrarch and <b>Giovanni Boccaccio</b>, made the Tuscan dialect of Florence the basis of literary Italian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Petrarch’s friend <b>Giovanni Boccaccio</b>, author of the Decameron, wrote a Latin encyclopedia of the pagan gods and lodged the Calabrian scholar Leontius Pilatus in Florence while he made the first Latin translation of Homer, around 1360.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Dante, Petrarch, <b>Boccaccio</b>, Machiavelli and Guicciardini expressed a desire to see Italy become a unified nation, and in 1764 Gian Rinaldo Carli's Della Patria degli Italiani reflected a sense of Italian national identity.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…wrote it in Tuscan, and so was one of the first in the Middle Ages to treat a serious subject, the Redemption of humanity, in the low and “vulgar” Italian language rather than in Latin; <b>Boccaccio</b>'s account that Dante began an early version in Latin is still controversial.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1354 Nicholas Sigeros gave Petrarch manuscripts of the Iliad and the Odyssey, and <b>Giovanni Boccaccio</b> persuaded the monk Leontius Pilatus to translate them into Latin prose; he finished the Iliad and came close to finishing the Odyssey.</blockquote><a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Certaldo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1353" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Giovanni Boccaccio"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1353">What was here in 1353? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A kindly thing it is to have compassion of the afflicted and albeit it well beseemeth every one, yet of those is it more particularly required who have erst had need of comfort and have found it in any…&#8221;</p><footer>The Decameron, proem &#183; tr. John Payne, 1886 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23700/pg23700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…vast trenches, wherein those who came after were laid by the hundred and being heaped up therein by layers, as goods are stowed aboard ship, were covered with a little earth, till such time as they reached the top of the trench.&#8221;</p><footer>The Decameron, day 1, introduction (the plague in Florence, 1348) &#183; tr. John Payne, 1886 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23700/pg23700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Who knoweth not that fire is most useful, nay, necessary to mortals? Shall we say, because it burneth houses and villages and cities, that it is naught?&#8221;</p><footer>The Decameron, the author's conclusion &#183; tr. John Payne, 1886 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23700/pg23700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Again, such as they are, these stories, like everything else, can both harm and profit, according to the disposition of the listener.&#8221;</p><footer>The Decameron, the author's conclusion &#183; tr. John Payne, 1886 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23700/pg23700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No field was ever so well tilled but therein or nettles or thistles or somewhat of briers or other weeds might be found mingled with the better herbs.&#8221;</p><footer>The Decameron, the author's conclusion &#183; tr. John Payne, 1886 &#183; answering readers who found some stories had been better left out &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23700/pg23700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Algates, whoso goeth reading among these, let him leave those which offend and read those which divert.&#8221;</p><footer>The Decameron, the author's conclusion &#183; tr. John Payne, 1886 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23700/pg23700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
